<%# Why the last save did not take, on the one field that can refuse one. #440.

    Amber because this app warns in amber and nothing else (#214), and a save that did not
    happen is a warning: the lifter is looking at a form they believe they have submitted.

    role="status" rather than alert, matching the exercise page: it is the outcome of
    something just asked for rather than an interruption. Read once and gone, so a reload
    does not re-announce a refusal from ten minutes ago. %>
<%- if @notice %>
  <p role="status" class="mb-4 rounded-md border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-800">
    <%= @notice %>
  </p>
<%- end %>
<form action="/workouts" method="post">
  <%== csrf_tag('/workouts') %>
  <div>
    <label for="date" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Date</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <%# The platform's own date control, and nothing else. #524.

          This was a `type="text"` box with Flowbite's datepicker bound to it, pulled from
          cdnjs at the top of this file on every render of both pages -- the only third-party
          script in the app besides the analytics tag, and carrying no integrity hash, so the
          page ran whatever that URL happened to serve. When it could not be fetched the
          input degraded to a bare text box demanding `09/23/2026` typed by hand, beside a
          calendar icon that no longer opened anything and said nothing about why. #516 shipped
          a banner that tells a lifter plainly when a tap did not reach the server; a basement
          is exactly where a session gets written down, and this field went quiet about the
          same failure on the same floor.

          `type="date"` is the control the phone already has. iOS offers its own wheel, which
          a `type="text"` field can never ask for; there is nothing to fetch, so it works with
          no connection at all; and it posts `YYYY-MM-DD`, which is one date in every locale
          and not the ambiguity #440 was about.

          The icon, its absolutely positioned wrapper and the `relative max-w-sm` box around
          all of it are gone with the script. They existed to decorate a text input into
          looking like a date one, and a native date input draws its own affordance -- keeping
          a second calendar glyph that opens nothing is the offline failure above, painted on
          permanently.

          The class list is field_style now, which is the rest of what Flowbite left behind:
          a blue-500 focus ring in an app that rings sky-800 since #333, and six `dark:`
          variants for a theme that does not exist, which drew one grey input in the middle of
          a page layout.erb keeps at bg-gray-100. Those were corrected by hand twice. The
          argument for holding this field out of the helper was that the rest of the input was
          Flowbite's own shape; the shape has gone, so the field joins the other eleven and
          cannot drift off them again. min-h-11 and text-base rather than the sm:text-sm the
          name field carries: this is a control a thumb taps to open a picker, and iOS zooms
          the whole page in on focus for anything under 16px. The goal date on the exercise
          page is already exactly this, and was the only native date input in the app until
          now.

          The value goes through Workout::FORM_DATE rather than a literal, which is #440's
          arrangement and the reason it survives a change of format: the view renders with the
          constant and the route parses with the same one, so the two ends cannot drift into
          disagreeing about which number is the month. The constant is `%Y-%m-%d` now because
          that is the only thing this input will take -- a value it cannot read it shows as an
          empty box, so rendering anything else here would open the edit page blank and lose
          the date on the next save. %>
      <%# The session's own date, not today's. #440.

          This form is shared by new and edit, and the value was Date.today unconditionally
          -- so the edit page opened with the wrong date in the box every time, and since
          the field is required and always posted, saving *any* edit moved the session to
          today. Renaming a session from three weeks ago dragged it and its twelve completed
          sets onto the current day, silently. That is the whole of "a workout's date can't
          be corrected": it could not be set to a past date at all, and touching anything
          else destroyed the date it had.

          `@workout` is nil on the new page and set for every nested workout route, so the
          fallback is what makes one template serve both. %>
      <input type="date" name="date" id="date" required autocomplete="on"
             value="<%= (@workout&.date || Date.today).strftime(Tectonic::Workout::FORM_DATE) %>"
             class="<%= field_style %> block min-h-11 w-full max-w-sm text-base text-gray-900">
      <input type="hidden" name="id" id="id" value="<%= @workout&.id || nil %>">
    </div>
  </div>
  <%# Optional, and the placeholder says what it is for rather than what to type: two
      sessions on one date were the same word twice in a calendar cell, and this is the
      only thing that tells them apart. Not required, because most days have one session
      and making somebody name it would be a field to clear rather than a field to fill.

      A generated session needs nothing here: it reads its program day's focus when this
      is blank, so leaving it empty keeps the day's name rather than erasing it. %>
  <div class="mt-4">
    <label for="name" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Name</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <input type="text" name="name" id="name" autocomplete="off" maxlength="120"
             value="<%= @workout&.name %>" placeholder="Morning lift"
                    class="<%= field_style %> block w-full max-w-sm py-1.5 text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
    </div>
  </div>
  <%# How the session went, as opposed to what it is called. #310.

      A textarea rather than an input because the useful version is a sentence -- "bar felt
      slow, slept badly" -- and a single-line box invites two words. whitespace-pre-line on
      the record keeps the line breaks somebody puts between two thoughts.

      Blank clears, the same as the name above it, so a note written after a bad day can be
      taken back off rather than being permanent the moment it is typed.

      Only on a session that exists, which is #368: this form is shared between New workout
      and Edit workout, and on the new one it asked how a session went before anybody had
      done it. There is no answer to that question at that moment, and a box with no
      possible answer reads as a box you have failed to fill in.

      A session logged after the fact is the case this costs something -- the note is
      knowable then, and now takes a second step. That is the rarer way round, and the field
      is on the very next screen; asking everybody a question nobody can answer to save it
      was the worse trade. %>
  <%- if @workout %>
  <div class="mt-4">
    <label for="note" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">How it went</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <textarea name="note" id="note" rows="3" autocomplete="off"
                placeholder="Bar felt slow today, slept badly"
                class="<%= field_style %> block w-full max-w-sm py-1.5 text-gray-900
                       placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6"><%= @workout.note %></textarea>
    </div>
  </div>
  <%- end %>
  <input type="submit" value="Save" class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 block bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-center text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">
</form>
